1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,610
The better question came from a criticism in the monthly round of the association: what, exactly, does the course prove?

2
00:00:08,610 --> 00:00:14,228
“A member of your department could not answer that,” the reviewer wrote.

3
00:00:14,228 --> 00:00:24,756
“The blank in your form is not a modest pause; it is a hole.” She meant that the team had been writing around the problem.

4
00:00:24,756 --> 00:00:32,014
The team gathered in the department office with a picture, an album, and a slide of the campus in snow.

5
00:00:32,014 --> 00:00:35,763
“We are ready to dig,” Maya said.

6
00:00:35,763 --> 00:00:49,035
“The search has to be narrower.” A gradual correction replaced an early apology: they had been determined to prove too much, and determination is not a method.

7
00:00:49,035 --> 00:00:51,634
So they specialized.

8
00:00:51,634 --> 00:01:04,036
One member kept a drawer of index cards; another ran a calculation on a slice of the data; a third hunted for the sort of question that survives an oral examination.

9
00:01:04,036 --> 00:01:14,803
“Be receptive,” Professor Zhao said, “not combative.” The enterprise would suspend its louder claims till the sand settled.

10
00:01:14,803 --> 00:01:25,170
A marsh on the edge of the region had taught them the same lesson: water finds the lowest line, and so does a good question.

11
00:01:25,170 --> 00:01:35,307
A singer from the nobility of the student choir, an aggressive reviewer, and a boy with a nut allergy appeared in the notes of the same week.

12
00:01:35,307 --> 00:01:42,066
The team could not concentrate, and the upset was visible in every face at the table.

13
00:01:42,066 --> 00:01:45,585
“This is not a novel,” Lin said.

14
00:01:45,585 --> 00:01:52,828
“It is a report.” Her initiative was to cut the first page entirely.

15
00:01:52,828 --> 00:02:00,621
They wrote a new first line, which they would defend here and everywhere: every number carries a name.

16
00:02:00,621 --> 00:02:04,985
All of them signed it, and her name came last.

17
00:02:04,985 --> 00:02:09,569
“Could you say it more plainly?” the reviewer asked.

18
00:02:09,569 --> 00:02:20,746
“No,” Maya said, “but we can say it again.” A handy wood frame held the sheet above the desk, where visitors could not avoid it.

19
00:02:20,746 --> 00:02:28,969
His own book, which he had not finished, said the same thing; the number of pages meant nothing.

20
00:02:28,969 --> 00:02:37,073
This was the point they kept coming up against, and they would write it down until they could hear it.
